# Build and relaunch — drop-in Makefile section for a Mac app.
#
# Copy this WHOLE section and set the three variables it needs. This is the canonical
# copy: take a fix here and bring it back, rather than letting each project's copy drift.
#
# It needs SCHEME, BUNDLE_ID and a `build` target. Everything else is a variable below, and
# an override goes *above* this block, never after it: `?=` only defers to an assignment
# make has already seen, so a later plain `=` wins and silently changes what runs. Two
# overrides come up often:
#
#   CONFIGURATION = $(CONFIGURATION_DEBUG)   # project names its debug config differently
#   APP_BUNDLE    = $(APP_PATH)              # project builds without -derivedDataPath, so
#                                            # only `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings` knows
#                                            # where the bundle landed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Quit before launching, because `open` on a bundle that is already running activates the
# existing process rather than launching the one just built. The build goes green, a window
# comes to the front, and it is yesterday's binary — which reads as "my fix did not work"
# rather than "the app never restarted". Graceful `quit` rather than `kill` so the app gets
# to save; leading `-` so a first run with nothing to quit is not an error.
#
# The wait is not padding. `quit` returns before the process is gone, and `open` on a bundle
# that is still terminating fails outright with LaunchServices error -600 rather than
# launching — so without it the target trades a stale app for a broken one. `pgrep -x`
# matches the executable name, not the command line: `-f` would match this very recipe's own
# shell and spin until the timeout on every run.
#
# The wait alone is not enough, though, which is what the retry below is for: `pgrep` going
# quiet means the process is gone, not that Launch Services has let go of it. It holds the
# dead instance's ASN a moment longer, and `open` resolves the bundle to that ASN and reports
# -600 for a process that has already exited. Retrying beats lengthening the wait, because it
# costs nothing in the common case where nothing was running to begin with.
#
# APP_NAME is the *executable* name, which is what `pgrep -x` matches; BUNDLE_ID is who the
# quit is addressed to. Neither is guessed from the other — read BUNDLE_ID out of the pbxproj
# (`PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER`), because a scheme and its bundle id need not agree (R2Explorer
# ships as io.mgcrea.SwiftR2), and an id nothing answers to turns the quit into a no-op that
# hands back the stale binary this block exists to prevent.
#
# QUIT_TIMEOUT is in quarter-seconds, so 40 is ten seconds.
APP_NAME      ?= $(SCHEME)
CONFIGURATION ?= Debug
APP_BUNDLE    ?= $(DERIVED_DATA)/Build/Products/$(CONFIGURATION)/$(APP_NAME).app
QUIT_TIMEOUT  ?= 40

quit: ## Quit the running app and wait for the process to go
	-@osascript -e 'tell application id "$(BUNDLE_ID)" to quit' 2>/dev/null
	@i=0; while pgrep -qx "$(APP_NAME)" && [ $$i -lt $(QUIT_TIMEOUT) ]; do \
		sleep 0.25; i=$$((i + 1)); \
	done

# The path goes through a shell variable rather than being named twice: a project that
# resolves its bundle with `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings` instead of a literal path would
# otherwise run xcodebuild a second time just to retry the `open`.
#
# A project with a second launch target — a debug probe, a variant environment — takes `quit`
# as a prerequisite there too. It launches the same bundle, so without it that target
# activates whatever instance is already up and exercises the previous build.
run: build quit ## Build and launch the Debug app
	@app="$(APP_BUNDLE)"; open "$$app" 2>/dev/null || { sleep 1; open "$$app"; }

# Add to .PHONY:  run quit
